# `remove-restrictions` / `unlock` / `change-permissions`, judged by other people's readers

Run of `docs/research/remove-restrictions-readers.py` over the files a scratch
harness produced with the shipped `runPdfTool` — four settings (`remove-restrictions`,
`unlock`, `change-permissions action: set`, `change-permissions action: clear`) over
the nine outside-locked fixtures in `tests/fixtures/encrypted/` plus `plain.pdf`:
40 files. The harness is deleted after the run, like the flatten probe before it; the
rows below are what it left, and `tests/pdf/remove-restrictions.test.ts` is what
pins them.

Nothing in this file is this app's opinion. pypdf 6.14.2, MuPDF 1.28.2, libqpdf
10.9.1 (through pikepdf) and poppler 26.10.0 each open the produced file with **no
secret typed** — "the password was removed" is only evidence when nothing is typed —
and the fixtures at the bottom are then opened with their own secret, so the
before-picture is measured on the same terms.

Columns: `MuPDF asks` is whether a reader has to be given anything to open the file;
`MuPDF /P` is its view of the permission flags, and **-4 means every bit set, i.e.
nothing restricted**; `planted` counts the sheets still carrying
`Lighthouse Inventory 1887`; `sheet-1 ink` is the non-white share of a 72 dpi raster,
against the fixture's own 1.51 %; `libqpdf check` is qpdf's own syntax check;
`/Encrypt in bytes` and `hunter2 in bytes` are greps of the downloaded file for the
trailer entry and for the typed secret in the clear.

